1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,193
Here's how to reverse engineer Guru from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,193 --> 00:00:06,933
Claude passed all thirty-one checks, and found thirty gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:09,527
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:14,156
It copies the part of Guru a small team actually needs to stop answering the same questions in Slack.

5
00:00:14,391 --> 00:00:16,294
Every answer is a card, with one owner.

6
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:19,776
Each card says whether it's verified, and when it needs review.

7
00:00:20,117 --> 00:00:21,525
Anyone can flag a wrong answer.

8
00:00:21,781 --> 00:00:25,225
And every edit is a new version, with a note saying what changed.

9
00:00:26,317 --> 00:00:27,725
The core rule is trust.

10
00:00:27,981 --> 00:00:28,813
It's never stored.

11
00:00:29,005 --> 00:00:30,839
It's worked out every time a card is shown.

12
00:00:31,095 --> 00:00:37,427
A card turns unverified when its owner leaves, when someone flags it, when someone else edits it, or when its review date passes.

13
00:00:37,616 --> 00:00:42,179
Sam Okafor edits Ben's card on software purchases, so it waits for Ben to verify it.

14
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:45,618
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

15
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:49,052
The foundation, with people, teams, collections and sign-in.

16
00:00:49,223 --> 00:00:50,844
Cards, trust and flags.

17
00:00:51,015 --> 00:00:52,935
Search, insights and the export.

18
00:00:53,191 --> 00:00:55,957
Then notifications, review reminders and a Slack command.

19
00:00:56,162 --> 00:01:00,725
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

20
00:01:01,817 --> 00:01:02,692
Here's what it built.

21
00:01:02,969 --> 00:01:06,916
The VPN card shows who verified it, when it's next due, and what changed.

22
00:01:07,236 --> 00:01:10,710
Searching for laptop puts the verified card ahead of one whose owner has left.

23
00:01:10,941 --> 00:01:15,634
Insights shows six of ten cards verified, sixty percent, and who needs to review what.

24
00:01:15,848 --> 00:01:18,130
Ben's cards list the ones he must check first.

25
00:01:18,493 --> 00:01:22,319
And Compare shows the old VPN steps against the new ones, line by line.

26
00:01:23,417 --> 00:01:30,087
In one final run, on a fresh database, all thirty-one checks passed, along with one hundred and fifty-five automated tests.

27
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:33,113
But the more useful result is what broke.

28
00:01:33,412 --> 00:01:35,737
The build exposed thirty gaps in the spec.

29
00:01:36,014 --> 00:01:37,636
Places where Claude had to guess.

30
00:01:37,934 --> 00:01:40,725
Nothing said whether the last admin could be deactivated.

31
00:01:40,913 --> 00:01:46,267
Search could turn the word does into doe, because the spec never said when to remove stop words.

32
00:01:46,587 --> 00:01:50,363
And a card with a channel-wide mention could notify everyone in a Slack channel.

33
00:01:50,726 --> 00:01:52,717
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

34
00:01:53,817 --> 00:01:56,313
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

35
00:01:56,547 --> 00:02:03,715
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, a real Slack workspace, the hourly job on a real scheduler, or hosting.

36
00:02:03,885 --> 00:02:06,753
Those are yours to check before your team relies on it.

37
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:09,172
So is it worth building?

38
00:02:09,556 --> 00:02:10,836
Guru doesn't publish prices.

39
00:02:11,092 --> 00:02:15,952
Its pricing page says the cost is tailored to your organization, and scoped on a call with its team.

40
00:02:16,118 --> 00:02:22,177
If you want AI answers drawn from Google Drive, Confluence and Slack, or controls like SSO and SOC two, talk to Guru.

41
00:02:22,390 --> 00:02:27,113
If you just need one owned, current answer to every question your team keeps asking, build it.

42
00:02:28,183 --> 00:02:29,570
Everything is in one file.

43
00:02:29,890 --> 00:02:37,194
The data model, nineteen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all thirty-one checks, with the thirty fixes already in.

44
00:02:38,167 --> 00:02:41,559
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

45
00:02:41,559 --> 00:02:43,629
If you build it, tell me what broke.

46
00:02:43,629 --> 00:02:44,089
I'm Sam.

47
00:02:44,089 --> 00:02:45,067
See you tomorrow.
